# Example 25: Morphogen gradient - French-flag positional information (S2)
# A single morphogen emitted at one end of a 1-D cell strip diffuses and
# decays, forming a monotone gradient.  Each cell hosts genes that respond
# to different concentration thresholds; cross-repression carves the
# gradient into contiguous, mutually exclusive expression domains, the
# classic French-flag model (Wolpert 1969; Driever & Nusslein-Volhard
# 1988), realized synthetically in E. coli with a graded autoinducer by
# Basu et al. 2005 (Nature 434:1130-1134).
#
# The gradient physics below (source influx + on-lattice diffusion +
# first-order decay + per-gene threshold response) is driven by the
# Python API shown at the end; the HelixLang GRN threshold mechanism
# (grn.add_gene(name, threshold)) carries the per-gene morphogen
# thresholds.
#
# #promoter name=p_near strength=0.30      # highest threshold: nearest
# #promoter name=p_mid  strength=0.15      # mid threshold
# #promoter name=p_far  strength=0.05      # lowest threshold: farthest

#promoter name=p_near strength=0.30
#promoter name=p_mid  strength=0.15
#promoter name=p_far  strength=0.05

#gene name=near promoter=p_near
ATG GCT TAA
#end

#gene name=mid promoter=p_mid
ATG GCT TAA
#end

#gene name=far promoter=p_far
ATG GCT TAA
#end

#regulate near -> mid strength=-1.0     # gap-gene style cross-repression
#regulate near -> far strength=-1.0
#regulate mid -> far strength=-1.0

#config ticks=800 output=stdout
